---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/logic-arguments">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Philosophy. You need nothing from earlier lessons — you have
    been running arguments since you were four years old ("it's not mine, it's my sister's").
    What this lesson adds is the ability to <em>see</em> the structures you have been using
    in the dark, and so to check them before trusting them. Everything else is built here,
    step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>identify an argument inside a messy passage — separating premises, conclusion, explanation and decoration — and set it out in standard form;</li>
    <li>distinguish the three basic forms of inference: deduction (certainty), induction (probability) and abduction (inference to the best explanation);</li>
    <li>apply the two independent tests every argument must pass — validity (does the shape hold?) and soundness (are the premises true?);</li>
    <li>use the counterexample method to prove an argument invalid;</li>
    <li>recognise the named fallacies, formal and informal, and say precisely what each one gets wrong;</li>
    <li>use the philosopher's three distinctions for classifying statements: analytic/synthetic, a priori/a posteriori, necessary/contingent.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: extract before you evaluate. Nobody can judge an argument they have not first
    written down cleanly, and most bad thinking happens at the extraction stage — judging a
    mess instead of the structure inside it.
  </p>

  <h2>You already do this: your phone just ran an argument</h2>
  <p>
    Your phone dies an hour into the day. You find yourself thinking: <em>if the battery were
    healthy, it would last the day; it didn't last the day; so the battery isn't
    healthy.</em> Without noticing, you have just executed one of the oldest validated forms
    in logic — modus tollens — and bet money on it by ordering a replacement. Nobody taught
    you the name; the skill came free with reasoning. This lesson's purpose is to make that
    invisible skill visible: to name its parts, catalogue its reliable shapes, catalogue its
    unreliable ones, and — most valuably — show the two independent places it can fail.
  </p>
  <p>
    An argument, in philosophy, is not a quarrel. It is a structure: a set of statements —
    the <strong>premises</strong> — offered as reasons in support of another statement, the
    <strong>conclusion</strong>. The move from premises to conclusion is an
    <strong>inference</strong>, and logic is the study of which inferences actually hold.
    Seen this way, arguments are everywhere and quarrels are the least interesting place they
    appear. A doctor who orders a test has run an argument ("the symptom pattern is
    consistent with X, the test distinguishes X, therefore test"). A bridge engineer argues
    from load tables to girder thickness. A detective argues from fingerprints to a suspect.
    What philosophy adds is a vocabulary for saying precisely <em>why</em> one of these
    structures works and another collapses — a skill roughly as useful as knowing how to
    read.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pair each arguer with their structure" widget="match" config={logicArguersMatch} />

  <h2>Finding the argument: premises, conclusions, inferences</h2>
  <p>
    Arguments hide. Very few real arguments arrive in neat numbered form; they arrive wrapped
    in persuasion, anecdote and emphasis. To identify one, look for two things. First,
    <strong>indicator words</strong>: conclusions are announced by <em>therefore, thus, so,
    hence, it follows that, consequently</em>; premises are announced by <em>because, since,
    given that, for the reason that</em>. Second, and more reliably, ask: <em>what is this
    passage trying to get me to accept, and what is it offering as support?</em> Not every
    passage argues. A description reports; an explanation tells you <em>why</em> something
    already agreed is true ("the bridge collapsed because the bolts were counterfeit" — no
    conclusion is in dispute); an argument tries to establish that something <em>is</em>
    true. Rhetoric merely aims to make you feel. Mixed passages are normal: an opinion column
    usually contains one argument and three paragraphs of decoration. The logician's job is
    extraction.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file the passages — who is arguing?" widget="sort-buckets" config={logicPassageSort} />
  <p>
    Extraction ends in <strong>standard form</strong>: list the premises, then the
    conclusion, with each premise a single self-contained claim. Hidden premises — the ones
    everyone is assumed to know ("Tuesday, therefore the market is open" assumes <em>the
    market opens on Tuesdays</em>) — should be written in explicitly, because they are where
    arguments usually fail. The finished reconstruction can then be tested mechanically,
    which is what the widget below does for the most famous argument in history.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the classic syllogism, assembled step by step" widget="equationStep" config={sylCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> in the syllogism, the conclusion introduces no idea that
    was not already in the premises — it merely connects them, which is why accepting the
    premises while denying the conclusion is not permitted. The fourth card shows the
    conditional version of the same idea: given <em>if P then Q</em>, asserting P forces Q.
    When you read the notes, watch what each premise contributes: the first spreads a net, the
    second puts something in it, and the conclusion closes it. That division of labour is
    what "the conclusion follows" means.
  </p>

  <h2>Three forms of argument: deduction, induction, abduction</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Deduction</strong> trades in certainty. A deductive argument claims that
    <em>if</em> the premises are true, the conclusion <em>must</em> be true — the conclusion
    is already contained in the premises, the way a verdict is contained in a complete set of
    evidence. All humans are mortal; Socrates is human; therefore Socrates is mortal. If you
    accept the premises, rejecting the conclusion is not permitted. Mathematics and formal
    logic live here.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Induction</strong> trades in probability. It generalises from observed cases to
    unobserved ones: every swan I have seen is white; therefore all swans are white. The
    premises can be true and the conclusion false — Europe was certain about those swans
    until 1697, when Dutch sailors found black ones in Australia. That is not a defect to be
    fixed but the price of the ticket: induction is the only path to knowledge of the
    unobserved, and all of empirical science runs on it. An inductive argument is judged
    <em>strong</em> or <em>weak</em> (never valid or invalid), and strength turns on sample
    size, representativeness and how cautiously the generalisation is stated.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="fill the blanks — induction's own verdicts" widget="cloze" config={logicInductionCloze} />
  <p>
    <strong>Abduction</strong> — inference to the best explanation — reasons backwards. You
    see wet grass; you infer rain, because rain would explain the grass and no competing
    explanation does as well. Sherlock Holmes, despite his catchphrase, almost never deduces;
    he abduces. Medicine, detective work, debugging and the historical sciences (evolution,
    cosmology) are abductive at their core: we cannot watch the past or the microscopic, so
    we ask which hypothesis would best explain what we <em>can</em> see. The judgement of
    "best" weighs explanatory power, fit with background knowledge, and simplicity — which
    brings us to a famous razor.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="bet on what Holmes actually does" widget="predict" config={logicHolmesPredict} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "deductive" does not mean "good" and "inductive" does not
  mean "weak". They answer different questions. A deduction guarantees its conclusion only
  relative to its premises; an induction openly claims only likelihood. Judged by its own
  standard, a careful induction can be far more trustworthy than a deduction built on false
  premises.</p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that Sherlock Holmes "deduces" the criminal from
  clues. Deduction cannot introduce new facts — its conclusions are already inside its
  premises. Holmes works from effects back to the best explanation of them, which is
  abduction; his real method (and the real method of diagnostics, from medicine to car
  repair) is generating candidate explanations and eliminating the ones that fit the
  evidence worst.</p>

  <h2>Validity and soundness: two verdicts, both needed</h2>
  <p>
    Here is the distinction that does the most work in philosophy. An argument is
    <strong>valid</strong> if the conclusion follows from the premises <em>if they were
    true</em> — that is, if it is impossible for all the premises to be true while the
    conclusion is false. Validity is about the argument's <em>shape</em> only. An argument is
    <strong>sound</strong> if it is valid <em>and</em> its premises really are true.
    Soundness is about shape <em>and</em> truth. The upshot is that there are exactly two
    ways an argument can fail: a false premise, or a leaking shape — and the two failures are
    diagnosed by entirely different methods.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="drag the divider — the two verdicts" widget="compare" config={logicValidCompare} />
  <p>
    A worked example of each failure. Shape-failure: "All fish swim; whales swim; therefore
    whales are fish." Both premises are true, the conclusion false, so the shape leaks — the
    middle term (swimming) never connected whales to the class fish. Truth-failure: "All
    birds can fly; penguins are birds; therefore penguins can fly." The shape here is
    perfectly valid — yet the conclusion is false, because premise one is false (penguins
    broke it). The standard test for validity is the <strong>counterexample method</strong>:
    keep the shape, swap the words for others, and see whether you can make all premises true
    with a false conclusion. "All cats are animals; dogs are animals; therefore dogs are
    cats" shows the whales-fish shape is invalid once and for all.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that a valid argument, or even a logically flawless
  chain of reasoning, guarantees a true conclusion. Validity is a promise about
  <em>transmission</em>, not about <em>origin</em>: it guarantees only that truth gets from
  premises to conclusion undamaged. Feed a valid shape false premises and it will deliver a
  false conclusion with perfect efficiency — the penguins argument above. This is why the
  two questions must always be asked separately: does the shape hold, and are the inputs
  true?</p>

  <h2>Analogy, hypothetical reasoning, and Ockham's Razor</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Arguments from analogy</strong> claim that because two things are alike in some
    respects, they are alike in the respect at issue. Case law is built on them (this case
    resembles that precedent); so is much moral reasoning (a foetus responds to sound, as a
    newborn does; therefore it deserves similar protection). Analogy is a form of induction,
    and its strength is honest variable work: what matters is whether the <em>shared</em>
    properties are relevant to the <em>target</em> property. Two calculators made by the same
    factory will likely have the same failure rate; two books from the same publisher will
    not. "Same factory" is relevant to manufacturing defects; "same publisher" is not. Most
    bad analogies fail on relevance, not on number of similarities.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="find the faulty line — a gloss on analogy" widget="spot-error" config={logicAnalogySpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>Hypothetical reasoning</strong> works with conditionals — <em>if P then Q</em>.
    Two moves with conditionals are always valid: <strong>modus ponens</strong> (P holds,
    therefore Q) and <strong>modus tollens</strong> (Q is false, therefore P is false — "if
    it rained, the street is wet; the street is dry; so it did not rain"). Modus tollens is
    how hypotheses die: derive a testable consequence, show it false, and the hypothesis goes
    with it. And when several hypotheses survive, the tie-breaker is <strong>Ockham's
    Razor</strong>: do not multiply entities beyond necessity — prefer the explanation that
    posits fewer new kinds of thing. It is a preference, not a proof: nature is sometimes
    baroque. But as a research heuristic it has an excellent record, and it is what makes
    abduction disciplined rather than fantasy. (The razor is named for William of Ockham, the
    14th-century Franciscan, though the principle is older than he is.)
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="walk the conditional moves, valid and invalid" widget="equationStep" config={logicConditionalSteps} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> arguments are structures of premises supporting conclusions;
    they come in three forms that make three kinds of claim; and they are judged by two
    independent tests — the shape (validity) and the inputs (soundness). What remains is the
    rogues' gallery: the recurring ways arguments cheat.
  </p>

  <h2>How arguments cheat: fallacies</h2>
  <p>
    Some failures are so common they have names. <strong>Circularity / begging the
    question</strong>: the conclusion appears as a premise — "the Bible is true because it
    says so, and what it says is true." The argument has a valid shape but cannot persuade
    anyone who does not already accept the conclusion; it assumes precisely what is in
    dispute. <strong>Contradiction</strong>: a set of premises that denies itself ("P and
    not-P") entails <em>anything at all</em> in classical logic, so an inconsistent position
    proves nothing. <strong>Affirming the consequent</strong>: "If rain, wet ground; the
    ground is wet; therefore it rained" — invalid, because a sprinkler explains it too.
    <strong>Denying the antecedent</strong>: "If rain, wet ground; no rain; therefore dry
    ground" — same sprinkler refutes it. These four are the formal fallacies: failures of
    shape.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="audit a wet-ground argument" widget="branch" config={logicFallacyBranch} />
  <p>
    Informal fallacies fail on content. <strong>Ad hominem</strong> attacks the arguer
    instead of the argument — a liar can still state truths. <strong>Straw man</strong>
    refutes a claim nobody made, substituting a weaker version. <strong>False dilemma</strong>
    offers two options where more exist. <strong>Appeal to authority</strong> treats
    expertise as proof; it lends weight, not certainty, and only within the expert's field.
    <strong>Slippery slope</strong> assumes a chain of consequences without defending each
    link. The study of fallacies is not a debating rifle — real arguments are usually
    imperfect in ordinary ways — but a diagnostic manual for the specific diseases that
    reasoning most often catches.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that spotting a fallacy wins the argument, or even
  settles the truth. Naming a fallacy is itself a claim that needs defending — the pattern
  named has to <em>actually be present</em>, and some apparent fallacies are legitimate moves
  (expert testimony genuinely does support conclusions; some slopes genuinely are slippery).
  And even a proven fallacy only shows <em>that argument</em> fails to support its conclusion
  — it leaves the conclusion itself exactly as likely as it was. Another argument may yet
  establish it.</p>

  <h2>The philosopher's adjectives: analytic, a priori, necessary</h2>
  <p>
    Three distinctions classify statements, not arguments, and students mix them up
    constantly because they are independent of one another. <strong>Analytic</strong>
    statements are true in virtue of the meanings of their words: "all bachelors are
    unmarried" — deny it and you have said something confused, not something false.
    <strong>Synthetic</strong> statements are true in virtue of how the world is: "bachelors
    tend to die younger" needs checking. <strong>A priori</strong> knowledge is knowable
    without experience (arithmetic, pure logic); <strong>a posteriori</strong> knowledge
    requires observation (chemistry, history). <strong>Necessary</strong> truths could not
    have been false in any possible world (2 + 2 = 4; water is H<sub>2</sub>O, given what
    water is); <strong>contingent</strong> truths happen to hold but might not (there being
    seven continents; your current reading of this sentence). The default pairings —
    analytic with a priori with necessary, synthetic with a posteriori with contingent —
    mostly hold, though philosophers from Kant ("synthetic a priori") to Kripke (necessary a
    posteriori: the H<sub>2</sub>O case) built careers on the interesting exceptions. The
    distinctions matter in practice because they tell you <em>how</em> a claim can be
    settled: by analysis, by observation, or by considering other possible worlds.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Write in standard form the argument hiding in: "You should see a doctor about that cough — it hasn't cleared in six weeks, and a cough that lasts that long can mean something serious."</li>
    <li>An argument is valid but has a false conclusion. What exactly do you know about its premises?</li>
    <li>"If the alarm system is armed, the door is locked. The door isn't locked. So the alarm isn't armed." Name the form, and say whether it is valid.</li>
    <li>Give one statement that is necessary but known a posteriori, if Kripke is right.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Premise: your cough has lasted six weeks. Premise: coughs
    lasting six weeks can indicate serious conditions (hidden premise — supply it
    explicitly). Conclusion: you should see a doctor. This is an abduction — a best
    explanation — plus a practical conclusion. (2) At least one premise is false. Validity
    guarantees true premises would force a true conclusion; a false conclusion under a valid
    shape therefore certifies that the inputs cannot all be true. (3) Modus tollens —
    <em>if P then Q; not-Q; therefore not-P</em> — and it is valid: the door being unlocked
    eliminates the armed-alarm scenario. Compare "affirming the consequent", which is the
    invalid mirror image. (4) "Water is H<sub>2</sub>O." Given what water is, it could not
    have been anything else (necessary), yet discovering it took empirical chemistry
    (a posteriori) — Kripke's famous crossing of the default pairings.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>An argument is premises offered in support of a conclusion; extraction into standard form (hidden premises included) comes before any evaluation.</li>
    <li>Deduction claims certainty, induction probability, abduction best-explanation; each is judged by its own standard.</li>
    <li>Validity is about shape (could premises be true with a false conclusion?); soundness is shape plus true inputs; the counterexample method proves invalidity.</li>
    <li>Modus ponens and modus tollens are always valid; affirming the consequent and denying the antecedent are their invalid impostors.</li>
    <li>Fallacies are named failure patterns — formal (shape) and informal (content) — and naming one refutes the argument, not the conclusion.</li>
    <li>Statement-classifications are independent: analytic/synthetic, a priori/a posteriori, necessary/contingent — with celebrated exceptions at the joins.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Logic is the load-bearing wall of the rest of philosophy. The epistemology lesson asks
    when inductive inference is justified at all — the deepest problem in this subject; the
    ethics lesson's three theories are three families of arguments and live or die by the
    tests on this page; and the advanced modal-logic lesson formalises the "possible worlds"
    vocabulary this page only gestured at. Outside philosophy, the through-line to computer
    science is the story of the Going deeper section below.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Logic's history is the story of structure becoming machine. Aristotle catalogued valid
    syllogistic forms around 350 BCE; George Boole reduced the syllogism to algebra in 1854
    (<em>The Laws of Thought</em>); and in 1937 Claude Shannon showed that the same algebra
    describes electrical switching circuits — the digital computer fell out of a philosophy
    problem. Every <code>if</code> statement in every program is modus ponens wearing a
    compiler. Twentieth-century logic pushed further: Frege and Russell rebuilt arithmetic
    inside logic; Gödel (1931) proved that any consistent formal system rich enough for
    arithmetic contains true statements it cannot prove — a limit result that changed
    philosophy's self-understanding.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the working level, the modern method has two halves worth naming. <em>Expression</em>:
    translate an ordinary-language claim into symbols that keep only the logical bones — "if
    you want a ride to school, you'll need to be ready by 8" becomes S → R — because the
    bones are where validity lives. <em>Analysis</em>: interrogate the symbolised structure
    with three questions — what does it entail, is the set of claims consistent, and is the
    argument valid? Those three questions, run honestly, are most of what "thinking
    critically" turns out to be. On the ground floor, though, the craft is unchanged since
    Athens: extract the argument, put it in standard form, ask whether the shape holds
    (validity) and whether the inputs are true (soundness), and treat every shortcut around
    those questions as a suspect. The person who can do that reliably is very hard to fool —
    including by their own arguments, which is the harder and more valuable half of the
    skill.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Aristotle, Prior Analytics (c. 350 BCE); William of Ockham (c. 1323); Boole, The Laws of Thought (1854); Shannon, "A Symbolic Analysis of Relay and Switching Circuits" (1937); Gödel (1931); Kripke, Naming and Necessity (1972). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>

</TopicPage>
